# update6: the observability the next analysis round needs, chosen against named questions.
#
# Already present in base/update4 (no change needed): FUNCTION_TRACER, DYNAMIC_FTRACE, DEBUG_INFO_BTF,
# KPROBE_EVENTS, FTRACE_SYSCALLS, BLK_DEV_IO_TRACE, SCHED_TRACER, SCHEDSTATS, TRACER_SNAPSHOT,
# KALLSYMS_ALL, PERF_EVENTS, BPF_SYSCALL, BPF_JIT.
#
# Missing and wanted, each tied to a question this programme actually has:
#  * UPROBES + UPROBE_EVENTS - user-space function tracing. This is the instrument for the open
#    question "where do the 20-30 ms of session creation go": it can time llama.cpp's own functions
#    (load, graph build, provider init) without modifying them, and the vendor library's exported
#    entry points.
#  * OSNOISE_TRACER + HWLAT_TRACER - OS noise and hardware latency. These answer "is the board quiet
#    during a measurement" and "are there hardware stalls", which the programme currently answers only
#    with PSI and load average.
#  * IRQSOFF_TRACER + PREEMPT_TRACER + TIMERLAT_TRACER - maximum interrupt-off, preemption-off and
#    timer latency. With PREEMPT_DYNAMIC these quantify the jitter a host-side decode step can suffer,
#    which is the CPU-half question from the call-chain analysis.
#  * STACK_TRACER - stack traces on the above latency events, so a finding names a call path rather
#    than a number.
#  * HW_PERF_EVENTS - hardware performance counters on the X100 cluster (the AI cores remain invisible
#    to Linux), enabling cycle/instruction/cache-miss accounting for the host side.
CONFIG_UPROBES=y
CONFIG_UPROBE_EVENTS=y
CONFIG_OSNOISE_TRACER=y
CONFIG_HWLAT_TRACER=y
CONFIG_IRQSOFF_TRACER=y
CONFIG_PREEMPT_TRACER=y
CONFIG_TIMERLAT_TRACER=y
CONFIG_STACK_TRACER=y
CONFIG_HW_PERF_EVENTS=y
